// Contemporary novels appear only as titles (none are quoted).

const M1 = "Popularity tells you how many people bought it, not how much it rewards close reading.";
const M2 = "A long book isn’t automatically rich, and a short one isn’t automatically thin. Look at the sentences and the questions.";
const M3 = "A film shows the story was adaptable, not that the writing rewards study.";
const M4 = "Genre is a container. A mystery can have merit if its craft, characters and questions reward close reading.";
const M5 = "“Is Gatsby good?” ends in one word. Ask <em>how</em> or <em>why</em> the novel makes him so hard to judge.";
const M6 = "Finish reading early: leave days at the end to reread passages and gather quotations.";
const M7 = "The ISU asks for a novel you’ll study independently. Check the brief.";
